# collab-service

Live collaboration rooms for records: what one person types appears on
everybody else's copy, each field shows who is in it, and everybody with the
record open is counted. One room per record; the server holds nothing but the
open sockets, so a restart just makes every page reconnect.

It speaks the protocol the new ASC system's forms already use
(`apps/delta/records-service/src/features/collaboration`) plus one frame, so a
ASC Audit page on Domino, running the old validation engine, can have the
same feature.

## Why

Two people editing one ASC Audit record used to end with the risk score
wrong: each saved the whole page, the last save won, and the score it carried
was computed from that person's inputs, not from what both had entered. With
a room, every change reaches every open copy as it is made, each copy
recomputes its own score from the same inputs, and a save tells the others to
reload. Whoever saves, the inputs and the score agree.

## Rooms

`GET /rooms/domino/:host/:database/:unid` upgrades to a WebSocket. The
database segment is URL-encoded (`RCOA%2FNELAUAT2.nsf`); the UNID is the
record's.

Sign-in is Domino's own: the browser sends the session cookie with the upgrade
because this service is served under the same site (`collab.example.org`
is under `.example.org`, where `LtpaToken2` is scoped). The service cannot
read that cookie, so it asks Domino the way a browser would - no agent, no
design change:

1. `GET https://<host>/<database>/0/<UNID>?EditDocument` with the session
   cookies (nothing else from the header). The edit page comes back only to
   somebody who may edit the record: `access = edit`, and the page's own
   `uname` says who they are.
2. A 401/403 or a "not authorized" page means signed in but not an editor, so
   `?OpenDocument` is tried: the read page means `access = read`.
3. A sign-in form (Domino serves it with 200), a redirect or anything else
   means the session is not signed in: the socket is closed with 1008, which
   the client does not retry.

The answer is cached for `COLLAB_DOMINO_CACHE_SECONDS` per session and
record. Somebody with `read` is counted and may announce where they are, but
their changes and saves reach nobody.

## Frames

```
{ "kind": "patch",    "patch": { "by", "field", "value", "at" } }
{ "kind": "presence", "participant": { "id", "name", "mode"?, "field"?, "caret"?, "caretTo"? } }
{ "kind": "left",     "id" }
{ "kind": "saved",    "by", "at" }
```

Everything a member sends is relayed to everybody else in the room and never
back to the sender. `saved` is the addition: a page that posts the whole
record to its server sends it just before doing so, so idle copies reload and
copies with unsaved changes know theirs are already in what was saved. Frames
over 64 KB, or not of this shape, are dropped. When a socket closes, the
server says `left` for it, because a closing tab does not always get its own
farewell out.

## Configuration

| Variable | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `COLLAB_SERVICE_PORT` | Port, default 3040 |
| `COLLAB_DOMINO_HOSTS` | Comma-separated Domino hosts whose records may have rooms. Required; a host not listed is refused |
| `COLLAB_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` | Comma-separated page origins allowed to open a room, e.g. `https://www.example.org` |
| `COLLAB_DOMINO_CACHE_SECONDS` | How long an answer is kept, default 60 |
| `COLLAB_DOMINO_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` | How long to wait for Domino, default 10 |

`/health` reports open rooms and people.

## The page side

The validation engine (`packages/validation-engine` in the `asc` repo) joins
a room when the page's spec says so; off otherwise:

```js
collaboration: {
  enabled: true,
  url: function () {
    if (!window.docUnid) return ''   // a draft has no room until its first save
    return 'wss://collab.example.org/rooms/domino/www.example.org/RCOA%2FNELAUAT2.nsf/' + window.docUnid
  },
  me: { id: window.uname, name: window.uname }
}
```

## Not yet

Rooms for the new system's entries (`bearer` sign-in with the ASC JWT) are
still served by records-service. Moving them here is a change to that
service's socket route and to the room map (a shared bus rather than a
process-local map when there is more than one instance), not to the protocol.
